      "text": "To find \\(\\tan V\\) in the right triangle \\(VWX\\) with \\(VX = \\sqrt{13}\\), \\(VW = 3\\), and \\(WX\\) as the hypotenuse, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. **Identify the sides of the triangle:**\n   - \\(VW\\) is the opposite side to angle \\(V\\).\n   - \\(VX\\) is the adjacent side to angle \\(V\\).\n   - \\(WX\\) is the hypotenuse.\n\n   Given:\n   \\[\n   VW = 3, \\quad VX = \\sqrt{13}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Calculate the length of the hypotenuse \\(WX\\):**\n   Using the Pythagorean theorem:\n   \\[\n   WX^2 = VW^2 + VX^2\n   \\]\n   Substituting the given values:\n   \\[\n   WX^2 = 3^2 + (\\sqrt{13})^2\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   WX^2 = 9 + 13\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   WX^2 = 22\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   WX = \\sqrt{22}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Determine \\(\\tan V\\):**\n   The tangent of an angle in a right triangle is given by the ratio of the length of the opposite side to the length of the adjacent side.\n   \\[\n   \\tan V = \\frac{\\text{opposite}}{\\text{adjacent}} = \\frac{VW}{VX}\n   \\]\n   Substituting the given values:\n   \\[\n   \\tan V = \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Simplify the expression:**\n   To rationalize the denominator, multiply the numerator and the denominator by \\(\\sqrt{13}\\):\n   \\[\n   \\tan V = \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{\\sqrt{13}} = \\frac{3\\sqrt{13}}{13}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\(\\tan V\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{3\\sqrt{13}}{13}}\n\\]",
